{ignore}
<p>As a pair, <code>{markdown}</code> renders its content first - its fields and tags resolve
like anywhere else - and then reads the result as Markdown and writes the HTML. The text is
dedented first, so a block indented along with the template does not turn into a code block.
With a parameter instead, <code>{markdown $post.body}</code> writes the value of the parameter,
the way the <code>markdown</code> pipe does.</p>

<p>The converter is built in (<code>lib/markdown.php</code>), a CommonMark subset: ATX and
setext headings, paragraphs, emphasis and strong emphasis, code spans, fenced code blocks
(with a <code>language-</code> class) and indented ones, bullet and ordered lists (nested,
tight or loose, with a start number), links, images, autolinks, block quotes, thematic breaks,
hard line breaks and backslash escapes. Reference-style links, tables and footnotes are not
supported.</p>

<h3>Safety</h3>
<p>Raw HTML in the text is escaped, and a link or image whose address names a scheme other
than http, https, mailto, ftp or tel - <code>javascript:</code>, <code>data:</code> - keeps only
its text. The bare <code>html</code> option lets the author's own markup through; use it only
for text the application wrote. The HTML made from a value stays a value: a
<code>{php:getcwd}</code> in a post is shown, not run.</p>

<h3>Braces</h3>
<p>Because the content is rendered before it is read as Markdown, a <code>{</code> in it starts
a tag. A code sample with braces needs the <code>ignore</code> option, which keeps the whole
content as text - fields included. A pair that is never closed, and the single form without a
value, are strict errors.</p>
{/ignore}
